module domicile.mojom;


// The shell's control channel to the compositor, as a renderer-to-browser
// interface rather than a socket the page opens.
//
// This replaces a WebSocket to a loopback TCP port. The port was not a page
// server with a socket beside it -- it was the desktop's control plane, and
// `Spawn` on it is arbitrary commands on the machine running the desktop. A
// loopback port is reachable by every process on the box and no header can fix
// that; what fixes it is not having a port. See docs/architecture/
// ENGINE-FORK.md, "The page is served over a TCP port, and it should not be".
//
// The browser already holds a unix socket to the compositor. This interface is
// how the page reaches it without opening one of its own.
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE IS NOT THIS FILE. A renderer that is compromised can
// call anything here, `Spawn` included. What changed is *which* renderers can:
// the browser binds this only for a document whose origin is the shell's, so
// the capability follows the origin instead of following whoever can open a
// TCP connection. domicile:// is not web-safe and not CORS-enabled, so no
// ordinary web content has that origin.

// The members the fork keeps. The bands and copy-path protocol -- place_portal,
// remove_portal, declare_bands, render_band, claim_pointer, app_composited --
// is absent on purpose: layout positions the layer now and the page has stopped
// reporting where its own boxes are. See docs/architecture/ENGINE-FORK.md.
//
// Adding a member costs a near-full Blink rebuild, so they are added in
// batches rather than one at a time.

// A key combination: one the shell grabs, and a press or release of one.
//
// `keycode` is a Linux evdev code -- the same numbering `Key` forwards in, not
// the X keycode the Wayland keymap uses, which is this plus 8. The renderer
// resolves a chord grabbed by name to one of these; see
// DomicileHost::grabShortcut.
struct Shortcut {
  uint32 keycode;
  bool alt;
  bool ctrl;
  bool shift;
  bool meta;
};

// What a client asked the chrome to show over its own surface.
//
// An enum rather than the string it arrives as. `wp_cursor_shape_v1`'s shapes
// are a closed set, the compositor already has them as one
// (`domicile_protocol::CursorShape`), and carrying them across here as text
// meant nothing between the socket and the page ever asked whether a name
// named anything -- and an unknown CSS keyword is a no-op rather than an
// error, so the symptom was an arrow where a hand should be with nothing said.
// The wire names live in components/domicile/common/cursor_shape.h, which is
// the codec both ends of this go through.
enum CursorShape {
  kNone,
  kDefault,
  kContextMenu,
  kHelp,
  kPointer,
  kProgress,
  kWait,
  kCell,
  kCrosshair,
  kText,
  kVerticalText,
  kAlias,
  kCopy,
  kMove,
  kNoDrop,
  kNotAllowed,
  kGrab,
  kGrabbing,
  kEResize,
  kNResize,
  kNeResize,
  kNwResize,
  kSResize,
  kSeResize,
  kSwResize,
  kWResize,
  kEwResize,
  kNsResize,
  kNeswResize,
  kNwseResize,
  kColResize,
  kRowResize,
  kAllScroll,
  kZoomIn,
  kZoomOut,
};

// Which way up a monitor is bolted to the desk.
//
// Named for the counterclockwise turn the content takes rather than the one
// the panel did, which is the `wl_output` convention and the config file's: an
// output rotated a quarter turn clockwise needs its content turned a quarter
// turn counterclockwise to come out upright, and kRotate90 is that turn.
enum DisplayTransform {
  kNormal,
  kRotate90,
  kRotate180,
  kRotate270,
};

// Which way round the desktop is drawn.
//
// An enum rather than the string it arrives as, for the reason CursorShape
// above is one: the set is closed at both ends of the wire, and carrying it
// across here as text would mean nothing between the socket and the page ever
// asked whether a name named anything. The wire names live in
// components/domicile/common/theme.h, which is the codec both directions go
// through -- and this is the one type here that crosses in BOTH, because the
// shell has a toggle on it.
//
// TWO MEMBERS, AND NO kSystem. "Follow the system" is what a program running
// on a desktop offers; this engine paints the desktop.
enum Theme {
  kDark,
  kLight,
};

// One screen of the desktop.
//
// Integers, unlike the sizes above. Those come from a layout box, where a CSS
// pixel is fractional; a display comes from the desktop's configuration, which
// names whole ones. Its geometry is logical all the same -- a `wl_output` mode
// is `width`/`height` times `scale` -- and `scale` is what clients on the
// screen draw at, not the shell's own density.
//
// EXCEPT `mode_width`/`mode_height`, which are the pixels the panel scans out
// and are not a second spelling of the logical size: a monitor on its side
// scans out exactly as it did lying down, so a portrait 4K panel is a
// 3840x2160 mode and an 1800x3200 box. Neither is derivable from the other,
// because `scale` here is the INTEGER wl_output one and a 1.2 display's is 2.
struct DisplayInfo {
  string name;
  int32 x;
  int32 y;
  uint32 width;
  uint32 height;
  uint32 scale;
  uint32 mode_width;
  uint32 mode_height;
  DisplayTransform transform;
};

// One thing that was copied, as a row of the clipboard's history.
//
// A preview and an id rather than the text. The compositor keeps the bytes and
// a page hands the id back with CopyClipboardEntry, so what crosses into a
// renderer is enough to draw a row and no more -- which matters because a
// password manager's copy is a row in this list.
//
// The id is 32 bits because the compositor's JSON reaches this process through
// a base::Value, whose whole numbers are a signed int. Two billion copies in
// one session is a century of copying something every second.
struct ClipboardEntry {
  uint32 id;
  string preview;
};

// One icon in the system tray: an application's StatusNotifierItem, as the
// compositor read it off the session bus.
//
// `id` is what ActivateTrayItem names it by. `title` is never empty -- the
// compositor falls back to the item's own id -- and is what a shell labels the
// icon with. `icon` is a `data:` URL, and empty for an item whose picture the
// compositor could not draw. `bus` is the bus name the item answers on, and
// `menu` the object path of its `com.canonical.dbusmenu` menu there, empty for
// none: a shell drives the menu with its own D-Bus calls.
struct TrayItem {
  string id;
  string title;
  string icon;
  string bus;
  string menu;
};

// Which button clicked a tray icon, as StatusNotifierItem names the three
// things an item can be asked to do: Activate, SecondaryActivate and
// ContextMenu.
enum TrayAction {
  kPrimary,
  kSecondary,
  kContext,
};

// One button of a Notification.
//
// `key` is what InvokeNotificationAction names it by; `label` is what the
// button says.
struct NotificationAction {
  string key;
  string label;
};

// One notification: an application's call to org.freedesktop.Notifications,
// which the compositor serves on the session bus. A page's own Web
// Notification arrives as one too -- the browser shows it by calling that
// server.
//
// `id` is 32 bits for ClipboardEntry's reason. `body` is plain text: the
// compositor tells senders it takes no markup. `icon` is a `data:` URL, for
// TrayItem's reason, and empty for nothing to draw. `urgency` is
// "low", "normal" or "critical", carried as the compositor's word because a
// page reads it as a word. `actions` leaves out the press on the notification
// itself, which is `clickable`. `timeout_ms` is -1 where the sender left how
// long it stays up to the shell, and 0 for until it is dismissed. `time` is
// milliseconds since the epoch, on the compositor's clock -- a double because
// a base::Value's whole numbers stop at two billion.
struct Notification {
  uint32 id;
  string app_name;
  string summary;
  string body;
  string icon;
  string urgency;
  array<NotificationAction> actions;
  bool clickable;
  int32 timeout_ms;
  double time;
};

// Page -> browser -> compositor.
interface ControlChannel {
  // Hand the browser the page's end of the other direction.
  //
  // A method here rather than a second interface the page brokers for itself:
  // the client end means nothing to anyone who does not already hold this
  // channel, so brokering it separately would be a second place to get the
  // access control right, for no gain.
  SetClient(pending_remote<ControlChannelClient> client);

  // Run a command on the machine running the desktop.
  //
  // The argv form rather than a string, and not because it is tidier: a string
  // would have to be split by somebody, and the somebody would be a shell.
  // There is no shell in this path and there should not be one.
  Spawn(array<string> command);

  // Ask what in the compositor's index of the home matches `query`. Answered
  // with ControlChannelClient::Files, carrying the same query.
  //
  // The compositor matches, and only what matched crosses: the index is the
  // whole home, tens of megabytes of JSON on a real one. A file's contents are
  // read with CallSystem.
  SearchFiles(string query);

  // A system call: a file, a directory, a watch or a process. `request` is the
  // call as JSON, `{"call":"read_file","path":"..."}`; `id` names it in every
  // answer, ControlChannelClient::System. The browser wraps it as a
  // `system_request` line and reads nothing else of it; the compositor checks
  // it, the lock included. See docs/SHELL-SYSTEM-ACCESS.md.
  CallSystem(uint32 id, string request);

  // Answer portal request `id`, which a ControlChannelClient::PortalRequests
  // listed. `answer` is JSON with a string `kind`, `{"kind":"access"}`. The
  // browser wraps it as an `answer_portal_request` line and reads nothing
  // else of it; the compositor checks it. See docs/PORTALS.md.
  AnswerPortalRequest(uint32 id, string answer);

  // Put a row of the clipboard's history back on the clipboard. The id is one
  // a Clipboard message carried; the compositor holds the row's full text,
  // which the page never sees.
  CopyClipboardEntry(uint32 entry);

  // Click an icon in the system tray. `id` is one a Tray message carried. What
  // the click does is the application's.
  ActivateTrayItem(string id, TrayAction action);

  // Clear notifications: ids a Notifications message carried. Each
  // application hears its notification was dismissed. An id that names none
  // now is one its application closed while the click was in flight, and is
  // passed over.
  DismissNotifications(array<uint32> ids);

  // Press one of a notification's actions -- "default" for the notification
  // itself. The application hears `action`, if it offered it; what the press
  // does is the application's.
  InvokeNotificationAction(uint32 id, string action);

  // Which window has the keyboard. FocusChrome takes it back to the page.
  FocusApp(string app_id);
  FocusChrome();

  // Ask a client to close. Not a kill: the client decides, which is why a
  // window can refuse and show a save dialog instead.
  CloseApp(string app_id);

  // Where the page put an <app>, in the desk's CSS pixels. The compositor
  // gives the client the scale of the monitor holding most of it. It positions
  // nothing: layout does that.
  SetAppBounds(string app_id, double x, double y, double width, double height);

  // The desktop's own geometry, and the scale the page is drawn at.
  SetDesktopSize(double width, double height);
  SetDevicePixelRatio(double ratio);

  // The user picked a theme off the shell's toggle.
  //
  // THE ONLY MEMBER HERE THAT TELLS THE COMPOSITOR WHAT THE DESKTOP IS rather
  // than asking it for something. Everything else on this interface is a
  // request about a window, a device or a process; this is the page saying the
  // desk is light now.
  //
  // It has to go down rather than stay in the renderer, because a theme that
  // lived in the page would be a desktop whose panels went dark and whose
  // windows stayed light. The compositor is the process the desk's clients can
  // hear -- it answers the settings portal GTK, Qt and Electron read their
  // color scheme from -- and it is the process that read the theme out of the
  // config file to begin with.
  //
  // Answered with ControlChannelClient::ThemeChanged, to every chrome rather
  // than to this one: a desk of three monitors is three of these channels, and
  // a toggle clicked on one of them is the whole desktop changing.
  SetTheme(Theme theme);

  // Offer a passphrase at a locked desk.
  //
  // THE ONE MEMBER HERE THAT CARRIES A SECRET, and the note at the top of this
  // file is what keeps it one: the browser binds this interface only for a
  // document whose origin is the shell's, so no ordinary web content can reach
  // it. Nothing in this process logs the argument, and the compositor's own
  // `Passphrase` type refuses to print itself.
  //
  // Answered with ControlChannelClient::Locked, to every chrome rather than to
  // the one that asked, and only when the desk actually opened -- the same
  // one-path-that-decides arrangement SetTheme has, for a harder reason: a page
  // that cleared its own lock screen because it believed its own keystrokes
  // would be a lock anybody with the devtools could open. A wrong passphrase is
  // answered with nothing at all.
  Unlock(string passphrase);

  // Lock the desk now, whoever is at it.
  //
  // Answered like Unlock: with ControlChannelClient::Locked to every chrome,
  // and only when the desk actually shut. A desk with no lock configured has
  // nothing to shut, and answers nothing.
  Lock();

  // This page's old frame is held for `theme`: the desk's windows may turn.
  //
  // Called from inside the shell's wipe, once the frame it wipes away from is
  // captured. The compositor waits for every chrome, tells the windows, and
  // answers with ControlChannelClient::WindowsThemeChanged.
  ThemeCaptured(Theme theme);

  // A key combination to route to the page rather than to whichever client
  // has focus. It stops in the browser process, in ShortcutRegistry, which
  // `WebViewGuest::PreHandleKeyboardEvent` matches a guest's keys against.
  GrabShortcut(Shortcut shortcut);

  // Put the cursor where the page says, in the page's own coordinates.
  //
  // THE ONE MESSAGE ABOUT INPUT THAT GOES THE OTHER WAY, and it stops in the
  // browser process -- the compositor neither draws this pointer nor hears
  // about it. A shell whose focus follows the cursor has to take the cursor
  // with the keyboard or have the focus handed back by the next pointer event;
  // a page can read where the pointer is and cannot put it anywhere, so it
  // asks. What a page may move is the pointer over itself, which is
  // `PointerWarpTarget`'s to enforce and not this channel's to promise.
  WarpPointer(double x, double y);

  // Input forwarding. Coordinates are surface-local and keycodes are evdev,
  // because that is what a Wayland client expects to be handed.
  Key(string app_id, uint32 keycode, bool pressed);
  PointerMotion(string app_id, double x, double y);
  PointerLeave(string app_id);
  PointerButton(string app_id, uint32 button, bool pressed);
  PointerAxis(string app_id, double dx, double dy, int32 v120_x, int32 v120_y);
};

// Compositor -> browser -> page. One method, not a callback on Spawn: these
// arrive because a client did something, not because the page asked.
interface ControlChannelClient {
  // A window's title changed. `app_id` is the same id an <app> element names.
  AppTitled(string app_id, string title);

  // A window's desktop id changed: the client's Wayland app id, which names
  // its desktop entry.
  AppDesktopId(string app_id, string desktop_id);

  // A window exists. Size is absent until the client has committed a buffer --
  // a toplevel maps before it draws, and how big a Wayland client wants to be
  // is something it says by drawing. Believing a zero here is what opened
  // windows at nothing at all. `desktop_id` is empty until the client sets
  // one.
  AppAppeared(string app_id, string title, string desktop_id, bool has_size,
              double width, double height);

  AppResized(string app_id, double width, double height);

  // The smallest and largest a client will draw its window, in logical units,
  // with 0 on an axis for no limit -- xdg-shell's own spelling.
  AppMinSize(string app_id, double width, double height);
  AppMaxSize(string app_id, double width, double height);

  // A popup over the window or popup `parent_app_id`: its box is `width` by
  // `height` at (`x`, `y`) from that one's, in logical units. Sent again when
  // the client moves it; it goes with AppClosed.
  PopupPlaced(string app_id, string parent_app_id, double x, double y,
              double width, double height, bool grab);
  AppClosed(string app_id);

  // The cursor a client asked for over its own surface.
  AppCursor(string app_id, CursorShape cursor);

  // A grabbed shortcut fired. Named for the event rather than the struct,
  // which would shadow the type inside the generated class.
  //
  // A chord claimed by the shell is matched in the browser process by
  // ShortcutRegistry and never crosses the compositor's socket at all.
  ShortcutPressed(Shortcut shortcut);

  // A key came up in a `<webview>` guest of this page: `keycode` is the key
  // and the modifiers are those still held. Sent only for a claimed chord's
  // key or a modifier, either of which can let go of a chord the page holds.
  ShortcutReleased(Shortcut shortcut);

  // The seat's modifier state. One seat, outliving every window, which is why
  // a dropped key release latches modifiers into clients that never saw it.
  //
  // Which modifiers are held, not xkb's four masks: the compositor has already
  // resolved depressed/latched/locked against the keymap, and a shell asking
  // "is control down" cannot answer that from a mask without the keymap too.
  Modifiers(bool alt, bool ctrl, bool shift, bool meta);

  // The whole desktop, whenever any of it changes.
  //
  // Every screen every time, not a delta: the compositor re-describes the
  // desktop on a resize, a density change or a config reload, and a page that
  // had to reconcile deltas could be wrong about a screen forever after
  // missing one. An empty list is a desktop with no screens on it, which the
  // compositor does not send -- it describes at least one output -- but which
  // is an answer rather than a silence, and the page must be able to hold it.
  Displays(array<DisplayInfo> displays);

  // What matched a SearchFiles, and only that: `query` is the one answered,
  // `files` the front of what matched -- paths relative to the home
  // directory, sorted by the compositor, a directory ending in `/` -- and
  // `matched` how many there were in all.
  //
  // An answer to SearchFiles rather than a push. It is still a message and
  // not a reply: the page has one listener per message type and a callback
  // would be a second shape of delivery for one answer. The query comes back
  // so the page can tell answers apart.
  //
  // A home that could not be read sends nothing at all -- the compositor logs
  // it -- because a launcher drawing an empty list is how that failure would
  // otherwise look.
  //
  // `indexing` is whether the compositor's walk of the home is still running,
  // which is the difference between an incomplete answer and a wrong one: the
  // rows are a launcher's whole evidence that a file exists, so an answer from
  // an index still being built has to arrive saying so.
  Files(string query,
        array<string> files,
        uint32 matched,
        bool indexing);

  // What has been copied on this desktop, newest first.
  //
  // Pushed, unlike Files, and with no member on ControlChannel to ask for it:
  // the compositor is the process a
  // wl_data_device.set_selection arrives at, so a copy is an event it already
  // hears. Sent whenever the history changes and once more to a chrome that
  // has just connected. An empty array is a desktop nothing has been copied on
  // yet, which is an answer -- and the ordinary state of one that has just
  // started, because the history is in memory and never on disk.
  //
  // A page cannot read this for itself, and navigator.clipboard is the trap
  // that looks like it can: that API answers out of this browser's own
  // clipboard, which on the platform this engine scans out on is connected to
  // no Wayland client at all.
  Clipboard(array<ClipboardEntry> entries);

  // The system tray: every application showing an icon, in the order they
  // registered.
  //
  // Pushed like Clipboard, and the whole list every time: sent whenever an
  // icon arrives, leaves or changes how it looks, and once more to a chrome
  // that has just connected. The compositor is the StatusNotifierItem host --
  // the session bus is its, and a page has none. An empty array is a desk no
  // application has put an icon on, and every desk with no session bus.
  Tray(array<TrayItem> items);

  // The desk's notifications: every one not yet cleared, oldest first.
  //
  // Pushed like Tray, and the whole list every time: sent whenever one
  // arrives, is replaced or goes, and once more to a chrome that has just
  // connected -- the history is the compositor's, so a page that reloads
  // keeps it. Which are new is the page's to tell. An empty array is a desk
  // with nothing to say, and every desk with no session bus.
  Notifications(array<Notification> items);

  // Which way round the desktop is drawn now.
  //
  // Named for the event rather than the enum, which would shadow the type
  // inside the generated class -- the same reason ShortcutPressed is not
  // called Shortcut.
  //
  // Pushed like Clipboard, and the one pushed message whose cause can be this
  // page: it arrives when a chrome has just connected, when a reload of the
  // compositor's config moved `[theme]`, and when any chrome calls
  // ControlChannel::SetTheme. The sender is told again as part of that last
  // one, which is deliberate -- one path sets the theme, rather than a page
  // that believes its own click.
  ThemeChanged(Theme theme);
  // Whether anybody is at this desktop: true is a desk nobody has touched for
  // as long as its config says, false is somebody back at it.
  //
  // Pushed like Clipboard, and a state rather than an edge although the
  // compositor decides an edge: over there an edge is what a connector can
  // take, because lighting one is a modeset and a dark desk restating itself
  // every tick is a modeset a second. A page reloads, so it is sent where the
  // desk stands -- on the turn that changes, and once more to a chrome that
  // has just connected.
  //
  // A page cannot count hands for itself. A shell is the desktop, so its
  // document stays visible while the glass is off: every idle signal the web
  // platform has says somebody is here on a desk nobody has been at for an
  // hour. A desktop with no idle timeout sends none of these at all.
  //
  // NOT THE LOCK, and nothing here can be made into one: the seat is the
  // compositor's, so refusing to deliver what is typed is its decision rather
  // than the page's. That refusal is Locked below, which is a member of its own
  // because the two are not the same fact -- a desk can be dark and open, and a
  // locked desk somebody has just wiggled the mouse at is lit and shut.
  Idle(bool idle);

  // Whether this desk is locked: true is a desktop that will not deliver a
  // keystroke or a click to any client until somebody says the passphrase.
  //
  // THE COMPOSITOR HOLDS IT, which is what the whole thing is for. Input on
  // this system does not originate there -- the shell's page owns it and
  // forwards it over ControlChannel above, and the compositor injects it into a
  // Wayland seat -- so the lock is that injection not happening. A page reload
  // does not open the desk, an engine that died and came back does not, and a
  // page edited in the devtools of the browser drawing it does not either.
  //
  // The renderer keeps its own keys throughout, which reads like a hole and is
  // the opposite: the page is the thing forwarding, so it can draw a lock
  // screen and take a passphrase while nothing it forwards arrives anywhere.
  //
  // Pushed like Idle, and a state rather than an edge for its reason with the
  // stakes the other way up: the edge a reloaded page missed is the one that
  // would have raised its lock screen. Sent on the turn the answer changes and
  // once more to a chrome that has just connected. A desktop with no passphrase
  // configured sends none of these at all, because it cannot lock.
  Locked(bool locked);

  // Which way round the desk's windows are drawn now: ThemeChanged's other
  // half. It arrives once every chrome has called ThemeCaptured -- or the
  // compositor stopped waiting -- and the windows have had their chance to
  // repaint, and again when a chrome connects. The browser has already drawn
  // its own pages this way by the time it arrives; a shell holding its wipe
  // lets go on it.
  WindowsThemeChanged(Theme theme);

  // The config's accent color, contrast and reduced motion, which the settings
  // portal also serves applications, for the shell to follow too.
  // `accent_color` is "#rrggbb", or null where the config leaves it to the
  // shell. Pushed like ThemeChanged: once to a chrome that has just connected,
  // and again whenever a reload of the config moved it.
  AppearanceChanged(string? accent_color, bool high_contrast,
                    bool reduced_motion);

  // The shell's part of the desk's config -- its keybindings, each mode's, and
  // the options the config keeps for a shell by name -- as the one JSON line
  // the compositor sent, `type` and all.
  //
  // A STRING AND NOT A STRUCT, which is the opposite of every other member
  // here and is deliberate. A shell's `options` are whatever its config says,
  // and mojom cannot type a value nobody has declared. The renderer half of
  // DomicileHost reads the keys out of it, to resolve the chords grabbed by
  // name, and none of it reaches the page. The browser has already parsed it
  // once, as it does every line, so what arrives is a JSON object in valid
  // UTF-8 and nothing more is promised.
  //
  // Pushed like ThemeChanged: once to a chrome that has just connected, and
  // again whenever a reload of the compositor's config moved what it carries.
  ShellConfig(string config);

  // Focus moved, including to the chrome itself, in which case app_id is empty.
  FocusChanged(string app_id);

  // A client asked for the keyboard and has not been given it. The shell
  // decides: the compositor forwards the request with the seat where it was,
  // so a page that ignores this is a desktop where a background window cannot
  // take what its user is typing into. Never empty -- a request comes from a
  // client, and the chrome asking itself for focus does not cross the wire.
  FocusRequested(string app_id);

  // An answer to a CallSystem: a `system_reply`, `system_event` or
  // `system_end` line, as the compositor wrote it. The page parses it.
  System(string message);

  // Every unanswered portal request: the `portal_requests` line, as the
  // compositor wrote it. Pushed whole on every change and on connect. The page
  // parses it.
  PortalRequests(string message);
};
